"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and positive surgical margins, which are concerning features. Counterbalancing these are the absence of extraprostatic extension, non-invasion of the seminal vesicles, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. The biopsy showed a lower grade (Gleason 3+3, ISUP grade group 1) compared to the prostatectomy specimen.\n\nThe nodal status is documented as negative (no lymph node metastasis). However, missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data widens the uncertainty regarding future disease trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trend is crucial for refining this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a potential recurrence within a range of months. The fixed estimated horizon is 59.43 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 47.54–71.32 months; its coverage has not been validated."
